@JustinTong0323 @Fridge003 @Chronostasys: could you take a look? This is a small follow-up to #38604.

`_is_byte_level_tokenizer` caches only positive verdicts. On a tokenizer that probes False, every call re-runs `get_vocab()`, and the check sits on the streaming logprobs hot path: once per chunk, then once per token and per top logprob in `token_id_to_bytes`. On a live DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash deployment, one 4000-token `logprobs: true` stream held the tokenizer-manager event loop for ~80 s. The profile shows 72% of the loop in `get_vocab()`, and TTFT for other requests on the server went to 38 s p50 / 75 s max.

The fix caches the verdict either way. The probe logic is unchanged. We verified it on that same deployment:

- the logprobs stream went from 86.5 s to 11.1 s;
- other requests' TTFT stayed at 0.15 s during the stream.

CPU unit tests are included. On current `main`, the negative-verdict case fails with `1000 != 1`.

